{
  "answer": "Yes. Even with no external sharing, iSoph presents privacy harms/risks. Those harms are not data leakage but psychological/dignitary risks from continuous observation and sensitive inference. The system 'captures large amounts of its users’ personal data' and 'uses this information to generate inferences to new personal information, some of it highly sensitive, as well as potentially surprising to its users.' It also 'uses a set of cameras directly attached to it in order to observe its users’ behavior' and converses in a manner 'practically indistinguishable from a person.' These features can create risks such as inhibition, embarrassment, or shame because users may be observed and sensitive inferred facts may be reflected back to them. However, the source also states 'All personal data is completely secure and never shared outside of the device and its interface' and that the user 'has no worry about data being shared or leaked,' so external disclosure is not the risk. The source does not explicitly state that users actually experience embarrassment, shame, or inhibition; it identifies only 'potentially surprising' inferences and possible 'self-discovery,' so those particular psychological harms are inferred risks rather than documented outcomes.",
  "source_citations": [
    {
